    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Since these manuscripts are quite unique and often have very niche historical contexts, I'm particularly worried about the potential for things getting messy or difficult to track if we choose a system that isn't precise enough. In your experience, how do these two systems handle \"orphan\" items or collections that don't quite fit into standard categories? Is there a significant risk of the collection becoming unmanageable or disorganized over time?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Both DDS and LCC have provisions for handling \"orphan\" items, but LCC is generally considered more suitable for rare and unique materials. LCC's flexible, nuanced classification structure can accommodate unusual or eclectic subjects with specific notation systems, allowing for more precise control over classification. DDS, on the other hand, can become cumbersome when dealing with truly unusual or eclectic items, as it may require multiple sub-classifications, leading to classification disputes or inconsistency. Implementing a flexible cataloging system, such as AACR2 or Dublin Core, around LCC can also help to mitigate issues with orphan items.",
      "source": "target",
